WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.480
Ladders in machine knitting simply
means keeping needles out of work.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.240
You can see that the working yarn is

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:16.800
traveling across that empty space and
creating a column of holes or ladders.

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.480
A common use for ladders is mock ribbing.

5
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.560
The ladder gives the appearance
of a purl stitch.

6
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.560
Mock ribbing is a great alternative

7
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.840
to using a ribber or hand manipulating
purl stitches to create ribbing.

8
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.440
Needles out of work can also be used

9
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.640
on each side of cables. Again giving
the appearance of a purl stitch.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.120


11
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.080
Threading, ribbons, cords,

12
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.120
or even i-cord up that ladder can add
a decorative touch to your knitting.

13
00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.600
For an easy, vertical turning row,
leave a needle out of work.

14
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:01.920
And

15
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.080
when you fold the knitting,
you'll have a sharp edge.

16
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.960
You might use this for a
vertical band or a facing,

17
00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.160
for example. Just leave
a needle lot of work.

18
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.040
I hope you'll investigate ladders

19
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.840
and see how they can be a put
to use for your knitting projects.

